From 7e3f194b2025f37452457da506fd81886145598c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ggbdpq <ggbdpq@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 00:45:45 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] gateway: a Chat reply's leading <thinking> block is read as
 thinking
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A Chat reply may open its reasoning with <thinking>…</thinking> in the
text. thoughtTags held only <thought> and <think>, so neither tag matched:
chatDecoder.text took the block for the answer's own text and no thinking
block went out (#5106). In a recorded day on one OpenAI-compatible host,
47 of 2832 turns carried a literal <thinking in the answer's text, 44 of
them at offset 0 (neighbads).

<thinking> is added beside the two, closed by </thinking>. It shares no
whole prefix with <think> — they diverge at the seventh byte, 'i' against
'>' — so the wait in text covers the shared lead (< through <think>) and
no lead can name two opening tags. markedThought reads the same map, so a
Gemini-marked chunk opening with <thinking> is thought's text too.

afterThought, which strips a leading block for titles and descriptions,
keeps its own <think> — untouched here.

Test: TestChatThoughtTags gains the whole, split-tag, open-tag-alone and
never-closed <thinking> cases, red on the old map, green 20 runs in a row.

The tag and the two whole/split cases follow neighbads' patch on their
fork, verified here on top of main.

